\section*{Isoperimetric profile of the cubic flat three-torus}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Cubic three-torus isoperimetry}: The manuscript claims a complete isoperimetric profile for the unit cubic flat three-torus. Balls, tubes around shortest geodesics, coordinate slabs, and complements are the only minimizers, including the transition equality cases.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Cubic three-torus isoperimetry}: A reduction leaves nonstandard competitors to be excluded at two critical volumes. Geometric pattern classification and slice inequalities eliminate the three- and four-vertex possibilities except one parameterized case. Nested-area estimates reduce that case to positivity of an explicitly checked scalar expression, and the two-volume reduction recovers every volume and equality case.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Cubic three-torus isoperimetry}: The manuscript claims a complete isoperimetric profile for the unit cubic flat three-torus. Balls, tubes around shortest geodesics, coordinate slabs, and complements are the only minimizers, including the transition equality cases. The parameters, regularity assumptions and normalization in this question are retained in applying that assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 354 describes Lean coverage for the profile and equality-case classification in the unit cubic three-torus.

\textit{Cubic three-torus isoperimetry}: The manuscript links \texttt{CubicTorus.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

